<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chief Seattle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1780–<a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Seattle, Si'ahl, Sealth, Noah Seattle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seattle, usually styled Chief Seattle, was a leader of the Duwamish and Suquamish peoples. Born around 1780 to a Suquamish father and a Duwamish mother, he became a leading figure among his people and pursued a path of accommodation with the white settlers, forming a personal relationship with Doc Maynard. He was baptised in 1852 as Noah Seattle. The city of Seattle, in the state of Washington, was named after him, and a widely publicised speech arguing for ecological responsibility and for respect for Native Americans' land rights has been attributed to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chief-seattle.jpg" alt="Chief Seattle" width="330" height="507" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: E.M. Sammis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chief_seattle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Washington Territory, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Port Madison Indian Reservation, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.02,-117.99&amp;year=1835" aria-label="Washington Territory on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1782 513 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Chief Seattle"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2232 783h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2232 783h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.02,-117.99&amp;year=1835">What was here in 1835? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
